#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017} :activity/id #uuid "313df706-051c-53f8-a9d2-078969d05bee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777 :topic/id #uuid "bdc990ad-e824-598d-8c5d-0b62dd6e1501" :topic/math-academy-id 285} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065409 :tutorial/content "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}x+C \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." :tutorial/id #uuid "bfa0c70d-6cf7-5c69-8995-d9f6c980a4ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "49d02f63-57e0-5168-80dd-fdc3893de9af" :step/math-academy-id 1381 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064052 :question/id #uuid "a9671d61-6b85-5d84-b49c-a6443569f6c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-382"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisit